# Flowspec Specification Corpus — Decision Log
# Every significant decision made during the spec interview, with rationale.

---
decision: "Tree-sitter-first, no LSP"
date: 2026-03-10
area: architecture
rationale: |
  TDF 5-way synthesis. LSP is someone else's semantic layer — complete but
  opaque, heavy to manage (process lifecycle for 3 different language servers),
  and different for every language. Tree-sitter + custom semantic resolution
  gives Flowspec ownership of its analysis pipeline, uniform multi-language
  support, and the speed profile needed for incremental analysis. No external
  processes spawned during analysis. LSP was fully removed from the v1
  architecture — not even as optional enrichment.
alternatives_considered:
  - "LSP-first with tree-sitter supplementing (original design doc)"
  - "Tree-sitter-first with optional LSP enrichment"
  - "Pure tree-sitter, no LSP at all (chosen)"

---
decision: "Persistent in-memory graph with disk serialization"
date: 2026-03-10
area: architecture
rationale: |
  TDF 4-way synthesis. The original "no database" constraint was about avoiding
  external dependencies, not avoiding persistence. Single-symbol trace queries
  and incremental analysis inherently need a queryable, persistent data
  structure. Every successful incremental analysis tool uses this pattern —
  in-memory index with disk serialization. The YAML manifest is an export
  format, not the primary data structure.
alternatives_considered:
  - "YAML manifest as primary data (original design doc)"
  - "External database (too heavy)"
  - "In-memory graph with binary cache (chosen)"

---
decision: "Python, JavaScript, and Rust for v1"
date: 2026-03-10
area: architecture
rationale: |
  User explicitly requested all three for v1. Python first (Mozart is Python),
  JavaScript for broad applicability, Rust because Flowspec is Rust and
  enables dogfooding. Original design doc had Rust-only for phase 1.

---
decision: "Dual AGPL-3.0 + Commercial license"
date: 2026-03-10
area: constraints
rationale: |
  Matches Mozart's licensing model. Enables open-source use while supporting
  commercial integration. All dependencies must have AGPL-compatible licenses.

---
decision: "Rust implementation language"
date: 2026-03-10
area: constraints
rationale: |
  Tree-sitter has mature Rust bindings. Cargo workspace gives library + CLI
  split naturally. Single binary distribution. Strong serialization ecosystem
  (serde, bincode). Performance tier appropriate for incremental analysis.
  User confirmed after considering C/C++ (ClamAV background).

---
decision: "Failure-pattern diagnostics, not graph-theory terms"
date: 2026-03-10
area: diagnostics
rationale: |
  TDF 5-way synthesis. AI agents understand failure patterns better than graph
  theory. "Unwired feature" is actionable. "Isolated subgraph" requires the
  agent to figure out what it means. 13 diagnostic patterns identified,
  organized by what went wrong in terms agents can act on.

---
decision: "Confidence-scored diagnostics, false-negative-averse"
date: 2026-03-10
area: diagnostics
rationale: |
  User stated false negatives are the worst failure. Report everything above
  minimum confidence with confidence levels (high, moderate, low). Consumers
  triage by confidence. Never suppress a finding to reduce false positives.

---
decision: "Summary-first manifest with abbreviated field names"
date: 2026-03-10
area: manifest-schema
rationale: |
  Agents rarely dig past what's first presented to them. Summary section
  (~2K tokens) gives full structural overview. Abbreviated field names
  (vis, sig, loc) save tokens. Complete manifest for 50K-line project
  targets under 500KB.

---
decision: "stdout default output"
date: 2026-03-10
area: cli
rationale: |
  stdout is natural for AI agent consumption (capture and inject into
  context). --output flag available for workflows that don't support
  piping or redirects. All logs go to stderr to keep stdout pipe-safe.

---
decision: "Kill flowspec lint command"
date: 2026-03-10
area: cli
rationale: |
  Overlaps with diagnose. Layer violations and contract mismatches are
  diagnostic patterns, not lint rules. One command (diagnose) with
  --checks filtering is cleaner than two commands with overlapping scope.

---
decision: "89% test coverage target with adversarial testing"
date: 2026-03-10
area: quality
rationale: |
  User wants Mozart-level test discipline from the start. 89% coverage
  minimum. Every diagnostic must have true-positive, true-negative, and
  adversarial tests. Broken tests never deferred. Issues always filed.
  Agents must actively try to break their own work.

---
decision: "One multi-language fixture project"
date: 2026-03-10
area: quality
rationale: |
  Single fixture with Python, JS, and Rust code. Known properties per
  language for validation. More realistic than per-language fixtures —
  real projects mix languages.

---
decision: "Data-oriented architecture inspired by ECS"
date: 2026-03-10
area: conventions
rationale: |
  TDF synthesis. User's ECS instinct points at the right mental model:
  symbols as IDs in flat tables, properties as data, analyzers as functions
  that query data. Graph is passive data store, analysis logic is separate.
  Implemented as idiomatic Rust (not an ECS framework) — concrete types,
  traits only at real boundaries.

---
decision: "Linux primary, loose macOS support, no Windows"
date: 2026-03-10
area: constraints
rationale: |
  User's environment is Linux (WSL2). No macOS hardware for CI testing.
  Write portable code by default but don't spend effort on macOS-specific
  issues. Fix macOS bugs if reported and fix is straightforward. Windows
  explicitly not supported.

---
decision: "SARIF output format for CI integration"
date: 2026-03-10
area: integration
rationale: |
  SARIF (Static Analysis Results Interchange Format) opens deeper
  integration with GitHub Code Scanning, VS Code, and other tools.
  Added as output format alongside YAML, JSON, and summary.

---
decision: "Flowspec and codegraph-rust are fully independent"
date: 2026-03-10
area: integration
rationale: |
  Different tools solving different problems. Flowspec: batch structural
  analysis. codegraph: interactive knowledge graph queries. No integration
  path needed. Users can run both independently.

---
decision: "Async with tokio throughout"
date: 2026-03-10
area: constraints
rationale: |
  User explicitly requested async/tokio patterns. Async for I/O-bound
  operations (file reading, cache, watch mode). Tree-sitter parsing is
  CPU-bound — use spawn_blocking. Dependency policy: prefer mature,
  well-maintained crates, no hard limit on count.

---
decision: "Performance targets: <7min first run, <1min incremental"
date: 2026-03-10
area: constraints
rationale: |
  For a 50K-line codebase. Under 7 minutes ideal for first run, 10
  pushes it. Under 1 minute for incremental. Under 5 seconds for
  single-symbol trace from cached graph.

---
decision: "Defer duplication and asymmetric_handling diagnostics to v1.1"
date: 2026-03-27
area: diagnostics
rationale: |
  Both patterns have been carried for 10+ development cycles without progress.
  duplication (detecting repeated code patterns, near-duplicates, copy-paste)
  requires AST-level structural comparison — tree hashing and normalized
  subtree matching. The current graph architecture stores symbols and edges
  but not AST structure. Would require either storing normalized AST subtrees
  in the graph or re-parsing during analysis, neither of which fits the
  parse-once-query-many architecture.

  asymmetric_handling (detecting inconsistent error handling, missing cleanup
  paths, unbalanced resource management) requires control flow analysis —
  CFG construction and path enumeration. The current graph has call edges
  and reference edges but no control flow edges. Would require a new edge
  type or a separate CFG structure.

  Both remain on the roadmap as post-v1 work items. They are not removed —
  just explicitly deferred with documented rationale. v1 ships with 11
  active diagnostic patterns out of the original 13.
alternatives_considered:
  - "Implement partial detection with heuristics (rejected — high FP risk, violates confidence-scored diagnostics principle)"
  - "Remove from roadmap entirely (rejected — both are valuable patterns, just need infrastructure that v1 doesn't have)"
  - "Defer to v1.1 with documented rationale (chosen)"

---
decision: "Narrow v1 to one promise — complete, fast Python symbol references — and build a real resolver on tree-sitter"
date: 2026-11-10
area: scope, architecture
rationale: |
  Measured on Marianne (334 Python files) by an independent ast-derived oracle:
  flowspec found 309 of 380 certain direct callers; 136 of 137 missed call
  expressions needed the receiver's type (annotation, constructor, self,
  inheritance). 29 of 30 sampled diagnose findings were false, nearly all from
  references the graph did not see. A patch that added "possible caller" edges to
  the existing name-matching graph raised one trace from 343 MB to over 2 GB and
  an uncapped run exhausted the host. The consumer (the Marianne conductor) asked
  one question for three weeks, "which call sites reach this symbol", and grep
  answered it faster every time. The composer delegated this decision on
  2026-11-10 with the instruction: a good tool that does what it says on the tin.

  Decided:
  1. v1 promises one thing: for a Python symbol, every reference (call, read,
     import), with file and line, certain separated from possible, direct
     separated from transitive, and a statement of what was searched when the
     answer is empty. Everything else is frozen until that clears the bar.
  2. Tree-sitter only stays. No LSP, no external processes. Zero setup is the
     reason to exist, so flowspec owns a real resolver: per-scope bindings,
     import graph, class hierarchy with MRO, receiver types from annotations,
     constructors and attribute assignments.
  3. Analysis is indexed once and queried many times: persisted, incremental,
     .gitignore honoured by default, a hard memory budget that fails loudly.
  4. The bar is external and numeric, run in CI on real repositories: for
     caller/reference answers and for every diagnostic pattern, false
     negatives below 1% and false positives below 1% of labelled samples
     (composer, 2026-11-11); warm query under 2 s, cold index of a ~300-file
     project under 60 s, peak memory under 1 GB. The earlier draft of this
     entry said 98% recall; the composer's bar is tighter and replaces it.
  5. Diagnostic patterns keep running but a pattern is reported at high or
     medium confidence only after it clears 90% precision on labelled findings;
     until then its findings are low confidence and outside the default display,
     with a count line saying how many were held back. Nothing is suppressed.
  6. JavaScript/TypeScript and Rust adapters are frozen, not removed. Rust
     resumes when Python clears the bar.
alternatives_considered:
  - "Keep patching edges into the name-matching graph (rejected — the 11:27Z memory exhaustion is what that costs)"
  - "Wrap pyright/jedi or an LSP for Python (rejected — external process and setup; breaks the tree-sitter-only constraint and the zero-setup niche)"
  - "Keep the full v1 surface and fix breadth-first (rejected — eleven rounds of method-dispatch correction did not make method callers work)"
---
decision: "Make the Python index resident and bidirectional: export-table snapshot, forward posting list, patterns off the facts, then a watching process"
date: 2026-11-12
area: architecture, performance
rationale: |
  The composer pointed at the DDD structure they wrote for clamonacc
  (clamav/clamonacc/inotif/hash.c, 2015): a hash table whose entries are
  also tree nodes, so one key lookup lands on a node that already knows its
  parent and its children, and any subtree can be expanded or collapsed from
  that point. Its cost was paid once, at startup, by walking the tree; after
  that every operation was constant time on resident data.

  The reference index already has the key dimension (`(file, def)` through
  the export table) and the upward dimension (sites sorted by target). What
  it lacks is measured on Marianne, 323 files, warm, 2026-11-12:

    trace of a symbol           6.4 s, 379 MB   (0.06 s only when page-cached
                                                 and the budget check lands early)
    diagnose, any single pattern 6.4 s, 379 MB
    diagnose, data_dead_end      9.9 s           (forward scans over all sites)

  Stage timing (added 2026-11-12, `-v`) puts the warm cost where it is.
  Marianne, `diagnose --language python`, 6.4 s and 379 MB:

    legacy graph parse of all 323 Python files   1.7 s   (never used)
    index build, warm                            0.04 s
    index diagnostics                            1.5 s   (entity ids rebuilt per finding)
    manifest                                     0.05 s
    authority: graph projection + 13,311 graph
      losses + per-symbol byte ranges            2.6 s

  `analyze` on the same tree writes 34 MB and exits 3 (incomplete) because
  those 13,311 losses come from the graph parse the Python path never
  needed. The index is already resident-shaped; the process around it is
  not. `sites_within` is still a linear scan and every site carries a heap
  String for its reason; those matter next, not first.

  Decided, in this order, each measured on Marianne before the next:
  1. Python never builds the graph. When the selected Python files go to
     the index, the parse loop skips the Python adapter and the index
     supplies what the authority needs: custody (read/parsed/rejected from
     the index manifest), the finding proofs (already), the analysis-graph
     projection (entities and edges from the export table and posting
     lists) and the Python prerequisite losses the authority tests pin
     (`unresolved_type` on an unknown annotation, `unresolved_reference` on
     a dynamic namespace lookup, `capability_unavailable`/type_flows on an
     annotated contract, `unsupported_construct` on `match`,
     `unresolved_import` on a relative import the resolver cannot place),
     emitted from the facts at build time, once, not per process. This is
     also issue #37: with the graph gone for Python, a mixed tree sends
     Python to the index and the rest to the graph, and merges. Warm
     diagnose target: under 0.5 s and under 100 MB; `analyze` on a clean
     tree exits 0.
  2. Export-table snapshot (persist `Exports` with its derived indexes;
     validate the manifest by mtime+size before hashing) and a forward
     posting list. Store sites as struct-of-arrays (target, owner,
     line, one packed byte for kind, certainty and a reason code), with an
     offset array by target and a permutation sorted by owner, so "who do I
     reach" is an O(1) slice like "who reaches me". Reason strings become an
     enum rendered at output time. At the measured 385 calls and 813 loads
     per kLOC, 3 MLOC is ~2 M sites: ~35 MB both ways, half that delta-coded.
  3. Patterns read only the export table and the posting lists, with entity
     ids computed once per run. The three
     patterns that read per-file facts (phantom_dependency, missing_reexport,
     stale_reference) get the few per-file facts they need (names used per
     scope, `__all__`, import list) folded into the export table at build.
     Diagnose target: within 2x of trace, same memory.
  4. A resident process. `flowspec watch` (already in cli.yaml) keeps the
     index hot, re-extracts a changed file on an inotify event and
     re-resolves its import closure, and answers trace/diagnose/query over a
     local socket; the MCP server in integration.yaml sits on the same
     process. Startup cost returns to where DDD put it: once, at the front.
  5. Issue #37 (per-language split so mixed trees use the index) lands before
     step 4 so there is one engine to keep hot.

  Carried over from DDD on purpose: the key stays `(file, def)` and never a
  name, because 43% of method definitions share their name with another
  class; the name-to-ids list is a secondary index. The map is a sorted
  array or a studied hasher, not a fixed-size table: a code index knows its
  size at build time. Resident budget for 3 MLOC: ~50 MB export table,
  ~35 MB sites, buffers; ~100 MB, inside the mid-range laptop target with
  the memory limit idle.
alternatives_considered:
  - "Memory-map the existing bincode files (rejected — bincode is not a zero-copy layout; the decode is the cost)"
  - "Keep facts resident instead of snapshotting exports (rejected — facts are ~4 KB/file and 80% of the index; the export table is what queries need)"
  - "Build the daemon first (rejected — a hot process over a 6 s cold path still pays 6 s per restart and hides the per-process cost from CI users)"
